messages:
maria: quick heads-up — running into a weird combo of issues when using the JS SDK in Next.js middleware (edge runtime). imports are ESM-only, types blow up, and the runtime complains about sync fs calls. reproducible in a tiny repl.
jamie: ugh, can you paste the minimal repro? which Next version?
maria: Next 14 canary. minimal server: 
```js
// middleware.ts
import sdk from 'redwood-inference-js';
export const runtime = 'edge';
export default async function middleware(req) {
  const c = new sdk.Client({ apiKey: process.env.RI_KEY });
  await c.generate({ prompt: 'hi' });
  return Response.next();
}
```
error: "Cannot use import statement outside a module" when bundling for edge + TS type errors from "types/index.d.ts" referencing node builtin types.
tyler: hmm similar to other ESM/CJS interop things. In edge runtime we need the package to ship pure ESM + no node types. Do we have a shim for lib.dom/edge fetch?
sam: fwiw the quick workaround is to import runtime-only entrypoint: `import { edgeClient } from 'redwood-inference-js/edge'` — that bypasses node-only helpers. but types still complain unless you add `/// <reference lib=\"webworker\"/>` to your tsconfig or d.ts.
maria: that worked for the runtime, thanks. but onboarding is painful — the error isn't actionable for users. could we add a small note in the SDK README and an automatic fallback export?
jamie: agreed. we should: 1) add a dedicated `edge` entrypoint that is ESM-only and no-node. 2) publish a types-only package that uses `lib: ["esnext","webworker"]` ambient types for that entry. 3) provide a tiny DX snippet in examples/next-middleware.
tyler: ok i'll open a draft PR with the entrypoint and a README patch. will also add a TS suggestion to the error (when detected at build time) to point to `/// <reference lib=\"webworker\"/>` or the edge import.
maria: can we also detect Next middleware environment at runtime and throw a clearer error? like "Detected Next Edge runtime — use the '/edge' client or set runtime='edge' in your file"
sam: yeah add a friendly lint rule too in the example: `.eslintrc` snippet: 
```json
{
  "rules": {"redwood-inference/edge-client": "warn"}
}
```
jamie: tiny note: build-bot complaining in canary CI about the new export layout — file size delta. build-bot: pipeline failed on linux-canary (bundle-snap). see https://ci.internal/1234.
tyler: re: bundle size — the edge entrypoint should tree-shake fine. i'll make the edge package a separate export in package.json exports map so bundlers pick it.
maria: thanks. one more thing — typing: some users on GH reported errors about `Request` not found in Node when using the types in middleware files. adding the webworker lib to the published .d.ts should fix it.
jamie: great. assign PR to sam for the types bits, tyler for packaging, i'll add the example + docs.
sam: assigned and opened PR #1890 with d.ts patch. adding CI test that builds the example under Next canary.
build-bot: PR #1890 opened by sam, status: pipeline queued :hourglass: 
maria: awesome — this should reduce onboarding friction. i'll add a short note in the quickstart about "Edge middleware gotchas" and link to the example. :+1:
jamie: thanks team. if we can land this week it's high signal for developer experience improvements.
